steps.body:
Five minutes on your typing site. Home row first, then the punctuation this hour is built out of.

Type these three lines ten times each, without looking down:

#include <fstream>
std::ifstream fin("mixmilk.in");
std::ofstream fout("mixmilk.out");

Then these two, five times each:

fin >> c1 >> m1;
fout << m1 << "\n";

Two things to notice while your fingers learn them. The file name sits inside double quotes, not single ones, because in C++ single quotes mean one lone character and a whole file name will not fit in there. And the two chevrons come in pairs that show which way the numbers are travelling: out of fin and into your variables, then out of your variables and into fout. Every one of these goes into a real program in about fifteen minutes, and a key you have to hunt for is a train of thought you have to start again.

The account is a grown-up's, so they sign in and then step back. Everything after that is yours.

Find the problem yourself. Past contests list on usaco.org, the December 2018 contest, Bronze, and the first problem is Mixing Milk. Read the whole page out loud, including the boring part about where the input arrives and where the answer goes.

About those two file names. This is an older problem, from before the site moved to plain typed input, so the page names an input file and an output file. On this problem they are mixmilk.in and mixmilk.out. Read them off the page yourself anyway and copy them character for character, because a file name taken from a plan instead of from the page is the most expensive kind of typo there is: everything compiles, everything runs, and the grader finds nothing to read.

Now, before you open any old file: do not reopen your Python. Write your three lines on paper from memory, the way week 3 taught you. What the program reads, what it does over and over, what it writes at the end. If you cannot remember the pouring rule, go back to the problem page, not to your own old code. The point of the hour is that the plan lives in you and not in a file.

The only new machinery today. In C++ a file you read from is an ifstream and a file you write to is an ofstream, and both need #include <fstream> at the top. Here is the whole skeleton, and it is deliberately the whole of what this page will give you:

#include <fstream>
#include <algorithm>

int main() {
    std::ifstream fin("mixmilk.in");
    std::ofstream fout("mixmilk.out");

    int c1, m1;
    fin >> c1 >> m1;

    // your plan goes here

    fout << m1 << "\n";
    return 0;
}

Four notes on it. fin >> c1 >> m1; reads the next two whole numbers out of the file, skipping past spaces and line ends by itself, which is why the shape of the file matters much less than you might fear. That line reads one bucket only, and one line goes out at the end; how many buckets this farm has, and therefore how many of each of those lines you need, is on the problem page and is your first move. #include <algorithm> is there because the smaller of two numbers is std::min(a, b) and that is where it lives. And the line that says your plan goes here is the only instruction you get for the middle: the pouring is not printed on this page and will not be, because you already own it.

One type question worth asking rather than reflexing. Read the limits off the page. The biggest number in this story is a thousand million. The int wall you measured yourself in week 2 is a bit over two thousand million. So an int genuinely fits here and long long is not needed, and you should be able to say that sentence out loud with both numbers in it. Reaching for long long every time is a habit, and habits are not reasons.

Compile it with a plain g++ mixmilk.cpp -o mixmilk. Under MinGW on Windows that writes mixmilk.exe; under WSL it writes mixmilk with nothing on the end. If your shell says it cannot find the thing you just built, list the folder and use the name that is actually sitting there.

Then test it. Make a file called mixmilk.in in the same folder and type the page's sample into it exactly. Run the program from that same folder, because it asks for the file by its bare name and looks in the folder it was started from, not the folder it was compiled in. Then open mixmilk.out and compare it with the page's sample answer, line by line.

Then submit it, yourself. Untimed. Read the verdict whatever it says, and if it comes back red, find the letter, work out what that letter is telling you, and fix one thing. A rejection you can explain out loud is still a summit reached in weather.
